// Annex E.4.1: SPICE gives a subcircuit a multiplicity factor M that is never
// declared anywhere in the subcircuit. Since LRM 2.2 a module carries the same
// thing as the hierarchical system parameter $mfactor (§6.3.6, §9.18) — note
// this module declares no parameters at all, and $mfactor is still readable.
//
// WHAT IS PINNED. Table 9-29 fixes the TOP-LEVEL value of $mfactor at 1.0
// exactly, and resolves an instance as $mfactor_specified * $mfactor_hier. A
// device compiled on its own is the top of the hierarchy with nothing specified,
// so the product is empty and the value is 1.0 — not 0, and not "unset". The
// digit matters because §6.3.6 makes every flow contribution scale by it: a
// $mfactor that defaulted to 0 would silently stamp an open circuit, and one
// that defaulted to 2 would double every current in the netlist.
//
// WHAT IS NOT PINNED. §6.3.6 rule 1 ("all contributions to a branch flow
// quantity shall be multiplied by $mfactor") is deliberately NOT cited: the
// scaling is applied to the stamp, outside the analog block, and nothing this
// harness can assert observes it. A file that multiplied a probe by $mfactor
// and called that flow scaling would only be restating the bias directive.
//
// AND WHY $mfactor APPEARS ONLY IN THE `CHECK. §6.3.6's `badres` example writes
// `I(a,b) <+ V(a,b) / r * $mfactor;` and rules "the simulator will generate an
// error for this module", because the automatic scaling would then be applied
// twice; the same clause requires a warning for "a misuse of the $mfactor in a
// manner that would result in double-scaling". This fixture must COMPILE, so
// $mfactor may not appear in the right-hand side of a flow contribution here at
// all — not even multiplied by zero, which a syntactic double-scaling check
// flags exactly as it flags `badres`. The contribution below is therefore plain,
// and the `CHECK above is what reads $mfactor.
//
//! lrm E.4.1
//! lrm 9.18
//! bias V(p) = 2.0
`include "check.vh"
module annex_e_mfactor(p);
  inout p; electrical p;
  analog begin
    `CHECKX("$mfactor at the top of the hierarchy", $mfactor, 1.0);
    I(p) <+ V(p);
  end
endmodule
